{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774737,"nonce":"GGH0qOdvhG5veAiMp9YYj113l1A=","title":"Close the four pending tickets: UI tests in CI, a real merge gate, ADR-0059, per-path outputs","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #76 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `chore/pending-four` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-25T17:00:23Z · Merged: 2026-07-25T17:29:30Z\n\nWorks through the four tickets that were the actionable tail of the test-strategy\neffort, one commit each. `just test` green at every step (572/572 at the tip),\n72/72 vitest, 2/2 Playwright.\n\nNo workflow referenced `vitest` or `playwright`, so 72 no-DOM cases and 2 specs\nwere unreachable from CI — by the orchestrator's first rule they did not exist.\n`ui-tests` joins the required tier; `ui-e2e` starts on advisory probation\n(`continue-on-error`) like `kind` did. `just ui-test` / `just ui-e2e` added.\n\nTwo findings:\n\n1. **There is no required-check setting to flip.** Branch protection *and*\n   rulesets are both 403 here (private repo, free plan), so nothing GitHub-side\n   has ever blocked a merge over red checks. Demonstrated the cost: PR #72 merged\n   with `openapi-drift` red, and main has since been shipping a spec missing\n   `StepDto.inputs` (fixed in 4e4b288).\n2. **The kind tier's only failure in 9 runs was the harness, not the cluster.**\n   `poll` returns `DriveErr::Transient` on retryable workspace faults and the\n   scheduler re-polls; the tests `.expect()`ed it, so one `workspace: broken\n   pipe` reddened a build. 15 wait loops now ride transients out with a bounded\n   consecutive-error budget (-143/+85 lines).\n\nSo the gate is built where it can exist: `.github/required-checks.txt` (with a\n`required-if-run` tier for path-filtered jobs) + `just pr-gate \u003cn\u003e`. `kind` is\npromoted; `ui-e2e` stays advisory until ~2026-08-08.\n\nPer-run isolation now covers `admit`/`advance`, not just `reconcile_services`,\nand the teardown drains isolate per *message* — a `SUPERSEDE_TEARDOWN` payload\nthat no longer deserializes could wedge every run's tick permanently. Bounded by\na wall-clock deadline that dead-letters a run which cannot be ticked forward.\n`TickHealth` is in-memory per replica on purpose; the ADR amendment argues why\n(late dead-letter vs. premature dead-letter of a healthy run).\n\nRed-first: reverting the isolation fails 3 of 4 new cases with `a per-run fault\nmust never fail the whole tick`; reverting only the payload guard fails the 4th\nwith `BadPayload(\"missing field \\`attempts\\`\")`.\n\nThe \"blocked on CAS sub-tree addressing\" premise was wrong. The CAS is a\nper-file merkle store, so a tree already is a hashed list of `name -\u003e blob|tree`\nentries — selecting a subset is `tree_entries` + a bottom-up `put_tree`, sharing\nevery blob. `prune_tree` does it; declared paths ride the Pod so an adopted Pod\nprunes identically; a declared path the step never produced fails the step\n(`FailureClass::Config`) rather than becoming the silent narrowing the deferral\nexisted to prevent.\n\nOne optimization deliberately left (recorded in followups.md): egress still tars\nthe whole workspace before pruning, so a narrow `outputs:` saves storage but not\ntransfer.\n\n---\nDocs realigned: ADR-0059 → Accepted with an amendment resolving its open\nquestions, ADR-0007 amended, the false \"sub-tree\" claims corrected in\nfollowups.md and the s3 crate header.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}